                <pb facs="0258" n="138" sameAs="frus:frus1931v02/pg138"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">822.00/793: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Ecuador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Dawson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Acting Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Quito</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-08-25T08:00:00-05:00">August 25, 1931—8
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received 11:36 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>39. Colonel Larrea Alba appears to have the situation well in hand and the
                    support of the entire Army. Evidences of popular feeling against him decreased
                    visibly toward evening and the night passed quietly. Quito is quiet this morning
                    and the Legation has no reports of disorders elsewhere.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dawson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
